+@cindex BWK @command{awk} @subentry interval expressions in
+As mentioned, interval expressions were not traditionally available
+in @command{awk}. In March of 2019, BWK @command{awk} (finally) acquired them.
+Starting with version 5.2, @command{gawk}'s
+@option{--traditional} option no longer disables interval
+expressions in regular expressions.
+
+POSIX says that interval expressions containing repetition counts greater
+than 255 produce unspecified results.
+
+@cindex Eggert, Paul
+In the manual for GNU @command{grep}, Paul Eggert notes the following:
+
+@quotation
+Interval expressions may be implemented internally via repetition.
+For example, @samp{^(a|bc)@{2,4@}$} might be implemented as
+@samp{^(a|bc)(a|bc)((a|bc)(a|bc)?)?$}.  A large repetition count may
+exhaust memory or greatly slow matching.  Even small counts can cause
+problems if cascaded; for example, @samp{grep -E
+".*@{10,@}@{10,@}@{10,@}@{10,@}@{10,@}"} is likely to overflow a
+stack.  Fortunately, regular expressions like these are typically
+artificial, and cascaded repetitions do not conform to POSIX so cannot
+be used in portable programs anyway.
+@end quotation
+
+@noindent
+This same caveat applies to @command{gawk}.
+
+@node Non-greedy operators
+@subsection Shortest Match, or ``Non-greedy'' Regexp Operators
+
+Traditionally, regexps have always matched the leftmost, longest
+sequence of characters.  For example, given the text @samp{abxxxcd},
+the regexp @samp{x+} could match one, two, or all three @samp{x} characters.
+By defining a regexp to match the longest possible sequence, we know that
+@samp{x+} will match all three @samp{x}s.  Such behavior may
+be termed @dfn{greedy}, since a regexp will match as many characters
+as possible.
+
+The Perl language introduced ``non-greedy,'' or shortest-match operators.
+In the above example, @samp{x+} would only match a single @samp{x}.
 
-@cindex POSIX @command{awk}, regular expressions and
-@cindex @command{gawk}, regular expressions, precedence
-In POSIX @command{awk} and @command{gawk}, the @samp{*}, @samp{+}, and
-@samp{?} operators stand for themselves when there is nothing in the
-regexp that precedes them.  For example, @code{/+/} matches a literal
-plus sign.  However, many other versions of @command{awk} treat such a
-usage as a syntax error.
+The 2024 POSIX standard introduced shortest-match operators into POSIX
+EREs.  Syntactically, you create a shortest-match operator by appending
+a @samp{?} to one of the traditional regexp operators: @samp{*}, @samp{+},
+@samp{?}, or @samp{@{@}}.
+
+The shortest-match operators don't always match what you might think would
+be the smallest sequence of characters, since the general, leftmost-longest
+rule still applies to the overall regexp.
+
+To illustrate the differences, we use a program named @file{shortest-match.awk}.
+This program takes several strings, and replaces the text matching a given
+pattern with a single @samp{X}.  The program uses a number of @command{gawk}-specific
+features which haven't yet been described. We therefore delay presenting the
+code until @ref{Shortest match program}.
 
-If @command{gawk} is in compatibility mode (@pxref{Options}), interval
-expressions are not available in regular expressions.
+For each string, the program prints the following:
+
+@itemize @bullet
+@item
+The original string, in quotes.
+
+@item
+The pattern used with a shortest match operator and the result, indented.
+
+@item
+Further indented is a list of the start and lengths of the submatches.
+Submatch zero is the whole string, submatch one is the first
+parenthesized expression, and so on.
+
+@item
+The pattern used with a regular match operator and the result, indented.
+
+@item
+The same start plus length information for submatches of
+the long pattern.
+@end itemize
+
+Here is the output, with commentary:
+
+@example
+"aaaxxxzzz"
+    shortpat: /x+?/, result: "aaaXxxzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 1) -> "x"
+    longpat: /x+/, result: "aaaXzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 3) -> "xxx"
+@end example
+
+@noindent
+For the shortest match, only one @samp{x} is replaced.
+For the longest match, every @samp{x} is replaced.
+This is pretty straightforward.
+
+@example
+"aaaxxxyzzz"
+    shortpat: /x+?y/, result: "aaaXzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 4) -> "xxxy"
+    longpat: /x+y/, result: "aaaXzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 4) -> "xxxy"
+@end example
+
+@noindent
+In this case, the result is the same, since the longest
+possible string of characters is matched.
+
+@example
+"aaaxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxzzz"
+    shortpat: /(x+?)(x+)(x+?)(x+)/, result: "aaaXzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 16) -> "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
+        1: (s: 4, l: 1) -> "x"
+        2: (s: 5, l: 13) -> "xxxxxxxxxxxxx"
+        3: (s: 18, l: 1) -> "x"
+        4: (s: 19, l: 1) -> "x"
+    longpat: /(x+)(x+)(x+)(x+)/, result: "aaaXzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 16) -> "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
+        1: (s: 4, l: 13) -> "xxxxxxxxxxxxx"
+        2: (s: 17, l: 1) -> "x"
+        3: (s: 18, l: 1) -> "x"
+        4: (s: 19, l: 1) -> "x"
+@end example
+
+@noindent
+This case is more interesting.
+The same total amount of text is matched for both patterns.
+However, the text matched by the subexpressions differs
+between the two cases. In the longest-match case, the 
+longest possible subexpressions match first.
+
+@example
+"aaaxyxxyxxyxzzz"
+    shortpat: /((x+)(y+?)(x+))+/, result: "aaaXyxxyxzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 4) -> "xyxx"
+        1: (s: 4, l: 4) -> "xyxx"
+        2: (s: 4, l: 1) -> "x"
+        3: (s: 5, l: 1) -> "y"
+        4: (s: 6, l: 2) -> "xx"
+    longpat: /((x+)(y+)(x+))+/, result: "aaaXzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 9) -> "xyxxyxxyx"
+        1: (s: 10, l: 3) -> "xyx"
+        2: (s: 10, l: 1) -> "x"
+        3: (s: 11, l: 1) -> "y"
+        4: (s: 12, l: 1) -> "x"
+@end example
+
+@noindent
+This case needs careful examination. There is a @samp{+} on the outside,
+meaning that the total inner expression may match multiple times.
+For the longest-match expression, that indeed happens. The subexpressions
+two through four have the start and length values for the last occasion
+where the inner expression matched.  The shortest-match expression
+only matched the inner expression once.
+
+@example
+"aaaxyxxyxxyxzzz"
+    shortpat: /((x+)(y+?)(x+))@{2@}/, result: "aaaXyxzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 7) -> "xyxxyxx"
+        1: (s: 7, l: 4) -> "xyxx"
+        2: (s: 7, l: 1) -> "x"
+        3: (s: 8, l: 1) -> "y"
+        4: (s: 9, l: 2) -> "xx"
+    longpat: /((x+)(y+)(x+))@{2@}/, result: "aaaXyxzzz"
+        0: (s: 4, l: 7) -> "xyxxyxx"
+        1: (s: 7, l: 4) -> "xyxx"
+        2: (s: 7, l: 1) -> "x"
+        3: (s: 8, l: 1) -> "y"
+        4: (s: 9, l: 2) -> "xx"
+@end example
+
+@noindent
+Here, the results are identical; both the nature of the string
+to be matched and the POSIX longest-leftmost rule, cause this to happen.
+
+@c FIXME: Update this when the GNU matchers are finally gone.
+The MinRX regular expression matching engine, which is @command{gawk}'s
+default matcher (@pxref{Choosing Matchers}), supports the shortest-match
+operators.  The older GNU matchers do not.  Also, when invoked with the
+@option{--traditional} option, shortest-match operators are not supported,
+since traditional @command{awk} does not have these operators.
+
+@quotation NOTE
+The addition of a trailing @samp{?} into the syntax can lead to unexpected
+incompatibilities with previous versions of @command{gawk}. For example,
+consider a regexp like @samp{a@{1,2@}?b+}.  With the GNU matchers,
+this matches an optional occurrence of one or two @code{a} characters
+followed by one or more @code{b} characters.  With the new POSIX syntax,
+this is a non-greedy match of one or two @code{a} characters followed
+by one or more @code{b} characters.
+
+Technically speaking, using one repetition operator immediately after
+another is undefined.  The correct way to write the previous regexp is
+to use parentheses: @samp{(a@{1,2@})?b+}.  This is clear and unambiguous.
+@end quotation
 
