+Hexadecimal integer constants have been part of the C language
+since the beginning.  The C99 standard added support for
+hexadecimal floating-point values.  This is
+a floating point number of the form
+[@code{-}]@code{0x@var{h}}[@code{.@var{hhhh}}]@code{p+-@var{dd}}.
+The @samp{p} introduces the exponent, and is @emph{required}.
+The radix point and following digits are optional.
+As with integer hexadecimal constants, both @samp{0x} and @samp{0X}
+may be used, and case in the letters @samp{a} through @samp{f}
+does not matter.
+
+Beginning with version 5.4, @command{gawk} supports hexadecimal
+floating point values, as constants in @command{awk} program source
+code, with the @code{strtonum()} built-in function, and with the
+@option{--non-decimal-data} command-line option.
+Here is an example:
 
 @example
-$ @kbd{gawk 'BEGIN @{ printf "0x11 is <%s>\n", 0x11 @}'}
-@print{} 0x11 is <17>
+$ @kbd{gawk 'BEGIN @{ print 0xdeadbeefp-1 @}'}
+@print{} 1.86796e+09
 @end example
 
