+@node Extension Sample Intdiv
+@subsection Integer Division For High Precision Integers
+
+The @code{intdiv} extension adds a single function named
+@code{intdiv()}, as follows:
+
+@table @code
+@item @@load intdiv
+This is how you load the extension.
 
-If the program dies prematurely, as might happen if an unhandled signal is received, a temporary file may be left behind.
+@cindex @code{intdiv()} extension function
+@item success = intdiv(numerator, denominator, result)
+Perform integer division, similar to the standard C @code{div()} function.
+First, truncate @code{numerator} and @code{denominator} towards zero,
+creating integer values.  Clear the @code{result} array, and then set
+@code{result["quotient"]} to the result of @samp{numerator / denominator},
+truncated towards zero to an integer, and set @code{result["remainder"]}
+to the result of @samp{numerator % denominator}, truncated towards
+zero to an integer.  Attempting division by zero causes a fatal error.
+Return zero upon success, and @minus{}1 upon error.
+@end table
+
+This function is primarily for use with arbitrary length
+integers; it avoids creating MPFR arbitrary precision floating-point
+values (@pxref{Arbitrary Precision Integers}).
+
+The following example program, contributed by Katie Wasserman, uses
+@code{intdiv()} to compute the digits of @value{PI} to as many places
+as you choose to set.  This program should be run with @samp{gawk -M
+-f pi.awk}:
+
+@example
+@c file eg/prog/pi.awk
+# pi.awk --- compute the digits of pi
+@c endfile
+@ignore
+@c file eg/prog/pi.awk
+#
+# This file and how to use it are described in the manual.
+# Therefore, we respectfully advise you to Read The Fine Manual
+# for more information.
+#
+# Katie Wasserman, katie@@wass.net
+# August 2014
+#
+# Adjusted to use intdiv extension, April 2026
+@c endfile
+@end ignore
+@c file eg/prog/pi.awk
+
+@@load "intdiv"
+
+BEGIN @{
+    digits = 100000
+    two = 2 * 10 ^ digits
+    pi = two
+    for (m = digits * 4; m > 0; --m) @{
+        d = m * 2 + 1
+        x = pi * m
+        intdiv(x, d, result)
+        pi = result["quotient"]
+        pi = pi + two
+    @}
+    print pi
+@}
+@c endfile
+@end example
+
+@ignore
